---
import ExcludedDocumentsList from "../../components/ExcludedDocumentsList.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import LegalDocument from "../../components/LegalDocument.astro";
import Navbar from "../../components/Navbar.astro";
import { EXCLUDED_DOCUMENTS_INTRO_ESIGN } from "../../config/legal-content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="E-signature Validity — Filosign"
	description="How Filosign thinks about electronic-signature evidence and legal suitability."
>
	<div class="min-h-screen bg-background font-manrope selection:bg-primary/10 selection:text-primary">
		<Navbar />
		<main>
			<LegalDocument
				title="E-signature Validity"
				description="This page explains Filosign's electronic-signature evidence model. It is not legal advice and does not decide legal suitability for your workflow."
				lastUpdated="May 2026"
			>
				<h2>Short version</h2>
				<p>
					Filosign is designed to support evidence used in many electronic
					signature workflows: intent, consent, attribution, record integrity,
					timestamps, and retention. Filosign does not decide whether a specific
					document is valid in your jurisdiction.
				</p>

				<h2>Intent and consent</h2>
				<p>
					Before signing, users should be told they are using electronic records
					and signatures. Clicking a signing control, completing required
					fields, and authorizing a wallet signature may help show signer intent
					for that workflow.
				</p>

				<h2>Attribution</h2>
				<p>
					Filosign connects signing actions to account information, the email
					associated with the signing session, wallet authorization, timestamps,
					and workflow records. Those records can help explain who completed a signing step.
				</p>

				<h2>Integrity and audit trail</h2>
				<p>
					Filosign proof packets are intended to explain the signing workflow:
					who sent the document, who signed, when events occurred, which
					records back those events, and whether settlement rules were attached.
					Encrypted document storage and on-chain proofs are designed to support
					tamper-evidence, not to replace legal review.
				</p>

				<h2>Jurisdiction matters</h2>
				<p>
					Not every document, transaction, or jurisdiction has the same
					requirements. Consumer disclosures, notarization, witnessing,
					qualified signatures, industry rules, or paper-record requirements may
					apply. Users are responsible for deciding whether Filosign is suitable
					for their workflow.
				</p>

				<h2>Documents that may not fit</h2>
				<p>
					{EXCLUDED_DOCUMENTS_INTRO_ESIGN}
				</p>
				<ExcludedDocumentsList />
				<p>
					Always consult qualified legal counsel to verify whether a particular document is suitable for electronic signature under local laws and regulations.
				</p>

				<h2>eIDAS and QES</h2>
				<p>
					Filosign does not currently claim qualified electronic signature status.
					QES/eIDAS-qualified workflows require additional identity, process, and
					provider requirements.
				</p>
			</LegalDocument>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
